WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.833
A/B testing.

2
00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:06.381
Offline evaluation says the new model is better on old data.

3
00:00:06.381 --> 00:00:09.000
A live test is the honest final exam.

4
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.300
Here is the setup.

5
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:14.400
Live traffic is split, randomized per user.

6
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.400
Most users keep the champion.

7
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:18.600
A fraction goes to the challenger.

8
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:26.667
Then we measure a business metric, like acceptance rate, not AUC, and promote only if the difference is statistically significant.

9
00:00:27.867 --> 00:00:29.767
Three rules keep the test honest.

10
00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:32.676
First, run a power analysis before starting.

11
00:00:32.676 --> 00:00:36.767
An underpowered test that showed no difference showed nothing at all.

12
00:00:37.067 --> 00:00:39.741
Second, randomize per user or session.

13
00:00:39.741 --> 00:00:43.400
One user seeing both models contaminates the metric.

14
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.955
Third, declare one primary metric.

15
00:00:45.955 --> 00:00:50.067
Checking twenty metrics until one is significant is p-hacking.

16
00:00:51.267 --> 00:00:52.833
Here is the verdict in code.

17
00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:56.520
We simulate two thousand four hundred sessions per arm.

18
00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:02.000
The champion accepts about eleven point eight percent, the challenger thirteen point one.

19
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.300
A hypothesis test compares them.

20
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.967
If p is below point oh five, we promote the challenger.

21
00:01:09.167 --> 00:01:11.600
But before running, do the power analysis.

22
00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:18.301
Detecting a one percent lift at eighty percent power needs about fourteen thousand sessions per arm.

23
00:01:18.301 --> 00:01:20.733
Most quick A/B tests are underpowered.

24
00:01:21.933 --> 00:01:23.267
Here is a real case.

25
00:01:23.567 --> 00:01:26.033
Your new reranker beats the old one offline.

26
00:01:26.333 --> 00:01:31.033
So you route ten percent of analysts to it, and track answer acceptance for two weeks.

27
00:01:31.333 --> 00:01:34.926
The result is plus one point three, with p of point oh three.

28
00:01:34.926 --> 00:01:37.400
You promote it through the registry alias.

29
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:39.440
Let's recap.

30
00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:42.100
Live traffic is the honest final exam.

31
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.300
Judge on a business metric, not an offline score.

32
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.233
Do a power analysis first.

33
00:01:47.533 --> 00:01:50.872
And promote only if the result is significant.

34
00:01:50.872 --> 00:01:52.033
Next up: MLflow.
